body:
If you want to force a dock to stay in an orientation, use the bitflag field `EditorDock.available_layouts`. That can prevent it from being made horizontal or vertical. Currently `add_to_bottom_panel` creates an EditorDock as a parent and sets its available_layout to `DOCK_LAYOUT_HORIZONTAL`, which prevents it from being moved (or made floating).

For reference, this is what `add_to_bottom_panel` currently does, I would start with that and see what you like from there.
```c++
EditorDock *dock = memnew(EditorDock);
dock->add_child(p_item);
dock->set_title(p_text);
dock->set_dock_shortcut(p_shortcut);
dock->set_global(false);
dock->set_transient(true);
dock->set_default_slot(DockConstants::DOCK_SLOT_BOTTOM);
dock->set_available_layouts(EditorDock::DOCK_LAYOUT_HORIZONTAL);
EditorDockManager::get_singleton()->add_dock(dock);
```

> There doesn't appear to be a way to put a dock back to the bottom after it has been moved to one of the side docks (when initially set to dock.default_slot = EditorPlugin.DOCK_SLOT_BOTTOM). 

Yes, there isn't currently a method to force move the dock to another slot. You can restrict the dock to the bottom panel and that appears to be your need.

For reference, the BottomPanel now inherits `TabContainer` (the buttons you see now is a themed TabBar) and the buttons no longer directly impact the bottom panel. (And are no longer visible in the tree, which is why shortcuts don't work with them). EditorDock was introduced rather recently so may not have all the features you have come to expect, but I do think having them is important. 

You'll notice that `add_dock` has a lot less arguments, and that is because those arguments have been largely moved over to the parameters of `EditorDock`.

Correction: I just looked at the shortcut code again and it is updated instantly if you modify the shortcut through EditorDock
